                <pb facs="0713" n="701" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg701"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.00 Statement July 16, 1937/2: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Belgium</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sussdorff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-19T15:00:00+01:00">July 19, 1937—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 19—11:22 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>54. Department’s circular July 17, 2 p.m. In the absence of the Belgian Minister
                    for Foreign Affairs, I discussed the substance of your statement contained in
                    Radio Bulletin 164 with the Secretary General of the Foreign Office. He stated: <list>
                        <label>(1)</label>
                        <item>that the principles set forth in the statement are almost integrally
                            those which have guided and continue to guide the Belgian Government in
                            its foreign policy;</item>
                        <label>(2)</label>
                        <item>that most of these principles have been stated separately by high
                            Belgian officials in public utterances;</item>
                        <label>(3)</label>
                        <item>that the Belgian Government is prepared in general to cooperate in
                            advancing the principles in question.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sussdorff</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
